---
/**
 * §03 · HERO — docs/06 §03
 *
 * AUDIT 1.3, verbatim: "The hero banner should function as a stationary image
 * slider where only the images transition, rather than the entire page moving."
 * So: the frame, headline, deck and buttons are fixed. Only the photograph and
 * its own caption cross-fade — the caption belongs to the image.
 *
 * The copy block carries the school's real motto, read off the crest ring on
 * the official logo artwork ("DUTY · DEVOTION · DISCIPLINE"). Text extraction
 * never surfaced it; it exists only in the emblem. It is the single most
 * Sunbeam-specific line available and it replaced a generic name-and-year
 * eyebrow that merely repeated the masthead.
 *
 * ⚠ IT IS NOW A TAGLINE UNDER THE DECK, NOT AN EYEBROW OVER THE HEADLINE —
 * the client asked for it there. Nothing took its old place: every candidate
 * for an eyebrow line was one this project would have had to write itself.
 *
 * ⚠⚠ THE HERO IS THE ONE SECTION ON THIS PAGE THE CLIENT HAS REVIEWED LINE BY
 * LINE. Four things in it are their explicit instruction and not this project's
 * judgement — the headline hierarchy ("Where Ballia's Future Leaders" dominant,
 * "Take Shape" noticeably smaller), the deck sentence verbatim, the motto as a
 * tagline below it, and the overlay light enough to leave the building visible.
 * Each is marked ⚠ at the rule or the markup that carries it. Read the note
 * before changing the number.
 */
import { school } from '../../data/site';
import Photo from '../ui/Photo.astro';
import chemPractical from '../../assets/photos/sunbeem-students.jpg';
import community from '../../assets/photos/sunbeem-2.jpg';
/**
 * THE CLIENT'S BANNER SET — src/assets/banners/, nine wide ceremonial frames.
 *
 * ⚠⚠ THE RULE FOR THIS ROTATION IS: EVERY SLIDE HAS STUDENTS IN IT. That is
 * what the hero is for — a visitor should see the school's own children and
 * know within a second what kind of place this is. banner5 was in the rotation
 * and broke the rule: twelve adults in suits on a dark stage holding copies of
 * a "Hamar Ballia" publication, with not one student in frame. Its alt text
 * also described "framed awards", which is not what anyone in it is holding.
 * It is out. Do not put it back without a reason that outranks the rule.
 *
 * ⚠ AND FIVE OF THE NINE ARE OUT FOR THREE DIFFERENT REASONS — an earlier note
 * here said they were all "the same two subjects again", which was true of two
 * of them and was then applied to the other three without looking:
 *
 *   banner2  DUPLICATE — the same council investiture as banner7, same stage,
 *            same students, one frame earlier.
 *   banner6  DUPLICATE — staff on stage with framed plaques, the same subject
 *            banner5 covered.
 *   banner3  UNUSABLE — two students at a podcast studio with a burned-in
 *            camera timestamp, "2024.11.16 16:22", in the bottom-right corner,
 *            under magenta and green studio lighting. A date stamp baked into
 *            the pixels does not belong on a homepage.
 *   banner5  NO STUDENTS — see above.
 *
 * The four in rotation from this set are banner1 (NCC on parade), banner4 (the
 * school gathered at its own entrance), banner8 (cadets on the Republic Day
 * stage) and banner9 (the KG convocation).
 *
 * ⚠ banner4 IS 1280px WIDE — THE SMALLEST OF THE NINE, and already 16:9, so a
 * 21:9 hero crop takes more off it than off the others. It earns its place
 * anyway: the school's name board is in the frame above a hundred and fifty of
 * its own students, which is the fastest "this is a school" in the whole set.
 * It is the first slide to re-export if better originals turn up.
 *
 * Adding another is one import and one entry in `slides` — the timer and the
 * caption cross-fade are derived from the array's length and neither needs
 * touching. (The dot indicators this note used to mention were removed; the
 * hero now advances on its own, by swipe on touch, or not at all while paused.)
 *
 * ⚠ THE REST ARE 1600px WIDE AND docs/07 § A2 ASKS FOR 2400px. At a full-bleed
 * 21:9 hero on a 1440 laptop that is fine; on a 27-inch monitor they are being
 * upscaled. Worth re-exporting from the originals if the school still has them.
 */
/**
 * THE DRONE FOOTAGE — the client's opening shot, and the reason this hero has
 * video in it at all. The brief was "the school from the air first, then the
 * students", which is a SEQUENCE, so it is expressed as the first two slides of
 * the rotation rather than as a splash screen or a loader. A loader would make
 * every parent checking an admission date wait through it.
 *
 * ⚠⚠ THE MASTERS ARE GITIGNORED AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. src/assets/videos/
 * holds ~55 MB clips at 1080p50 / ~22 Mbps; an earlier pair were ~100 MB and one
 * of those was over GitHub's 100 MB hard limit, which rejects the push outright.
 * What ships is public/video/hero-*.mp4 — 1600x900, 25 fps, CRF 32, no audio
 * track, faststart — about 3 MB each, roughly 1.2 Mbps. Re-encode with:
 *
 *   ffmpeg -i banner-1.MP4 -an -vf "fps=25,scale=1600:-2:flags=lanczos"  *     -c:v libx264 -profile:v high -crf 32 -preset slow -pix_fmt yuv420p  *     -movflags +faststart public/video/hero-1.mp4
 *
 * ⚠ TWO ENCODES PER CLIP. public/video/hero-N.mp4 is 1600x900 (~3 MB) and
 * hero-N-sm.mp4 is 960x540 (~1.1 MB); phones get the small one. Both are
 * regenerated by the same recipe with the scale filter changed.
 *
 * ⚠ THE POSTER IS A REAL <Photo> UNDERNEATH, NOT THE poster= ATTRIBUTE. That is
 * what makes the video optional: the still is responsive, AVIF/WebP, and always
 * painted, so it carries the slide on its own wherever the film never loads —
 * mobile, reduced motion, a blocked autoplay. The video fades in over it only
 * once it is actually playing. It is also why the LCP element stays an image.
 */
import heroFilm1Poster from '../../assets/videos/hero-1-poster.jpg';
import heroFilm2Poster from '../../assets/videos/hero-2-poster.jpg';

import bannerParade from '../../assets/banners/banner1.jpg';
import bannerAssembly from '../../assets/banners/banner4.jpg';
import bannerInvestiture from '../../assets/banners/banner7.jpg';
import bannerCadets from '../../assets/banners/banner8.jpg';
import bannerKg from '../../assets/banners/banner9.jpg';

/**
 * docs/07 § A2 Priority 1 — 21:9 or 16:9, min 2400px.
 *
 * ═══ WHY THIS IS TWO SLIDES AND NOT THREE ═════════════════════════════════
 *
 * ⚠ AUDIT 1.1 IS ANSWERED AT LAST — THE FIRST SLIDE IS A LESSON. The audit asked
 * for "students engaged in learning" and the header of this file recorded, for
 * three revisions, that no such photograph existed. One does: four students of
 * the school in its own uniform and lab coats, running a chemistry practical at
 * the bench, with the aim written up on the blackboard behind them —
 * "Verification of the law of conservation of mass in a chemical reaction",
 * which is the CBSE Class IX/X practical. Lab, uniform and blackboard are all
 * the school's own. It replaces the wide campus shot: a building says where the
 * school is, and this says what happens inside it.
 *
 * ⚠⚠ THE RoboWunder SLIDE IS GONE, AND IT IS A CROPPING FAULT, NOT A JUDGEMENT
 * ON THE RESULT. That file is a designed POSTER with its headline baked into the
 * pixels. A 21:9 full-bleed hero crop cut straight through the typography and
 * shipped the words "nbeam" and "ONAL ROBOTICS CHAMPI" across the top of the
 * page, competing with the actual <h1> underneath. The same fault is on record
 * in the header of Majors.astro. The RESULT still appears in full, and legibly,
 * on Achievements and on STEM & Robotics, where the poster is shown `contain`
 * and nothing is sliced off it.
 *
 * ═══ AND THEN THE CLIENT'S BANNER SET ARRIVED ═════════════════════════════
 *
 * Six slides now, not two. The four added are from src/assets/banners/ — see the
 * note beside their imports for why four of the nine and not all nine.
 *
 * ⚠ THE ORDER IS AN ARGUMENT, NOT AN UPLOAD SEQUENCE. Learning first, because
 * that is what a school is for and what audit 1.1 asked the hero to show. Then
 * the parade, the youngest children, the award stage, an investiture, and the
 * senior school together — so a reader who watches the whole rotation sees the
 * age range, the ceremony and the results, and never two adjacent slides that
 * look like the same photograph.
 *
 * ⚠ SLIDE 1 STAYS THE CHEMISTRY PRACTICAL. It is the only frame in the set
 * showing a lesson rather than an occasion, it is the one the audit specifically
 * asked for, and it is the one that loads `eager` — so it is what a parent sees
 * before any rotation begins. Do not demote it to make room for a ceremony.
 *
 * ⚠ EVERY SLIDE CARRIES A REAL `caption`. A slide with none falls back to its
 * `brief` and renders the "Needed · A2" tag over the photograph, which is an
 * internal authoring marker and must never reach a visitor.
 */
const slides = [
  {
    src: heroFilm1Poster,
    video: '/video/hero-1.mp4',
    alt: 'The Sunbeam School Ballia campus at Agarsanda seen from the air, its blocks around the central courtyard.',
    position: '50% 50%',
    tone: 'ink' as const,
    brief: 'The campus from the air — the establishing shot',
    caption: 'The Agarsanda campus from the air',
  },
  {
    src: heroFilm2Poster,
    video: '/video/hero-2.mp4',
    alt: 'A second aerial view of the Sunbeam School Ballia campus at Agarsanda, its grounds and buildings from above.',
    position: '50% 50%',
    tone: 'ink' as const,
    brief: 'The campus from the air — the grounds',
    caption: 'The grounds at Agarsanda',
  },
  {
    src: chemPractical,
    alt: 'Four students of Sunbeam School Ballia in uniform and lab coats running a chemistry practical at the bench, the aim written up on the blackboard behind them.',
    /* The blackboard is at the top of the frame and the students across the
       middle. 38% keeps both in a wide crop; 50% would cut the aim off the
       board, which is the half that makes this a lesson rather than a posed
       group. */
    position: '50% 38%',
    tone: 'ink' as const,
    brief: 'Students engaged in classroom learning — a practical in progress',
    caption: 'A Class IX/X chemistry practical, in the school’s own lab',
  },
  {
    src: bannerParade,
    alt: 'NCC cadets of Sunbeam School Ballia drawn up in ranks on the school ground as an officer and the school’s staff inspect the parade.',
    /* Ranks fill the lower two-thirds and the sky the top. 55% drops the crop
       past the empty sky without cutting the front rank's heads. */
    position: '50% 55%',
    tone: 'ink' as const,
    brief: 'A co-curricular moment — parade, sport or performance',
    caption: 'The NCC contingent on parade',
  },
  {
    src: bannerKg,
    alt: 'Kindergarten children of Sunbeam School Ballia in convocation sashes and caps, standing in rows with their teachers.',
    position: '50% 46%',
    tone: 'maroon' as const,
    brief: 'The youngest year groups — a ceremony of their own',
    caption: 'The KG convocation',
  },
  {
    src: bannerCadets,
    alt: 'NCC cadets of Sunbeam School Ballia in uniform, standing in rows on the school stage with two of their officers, in front of a Republic Day backdrop.',
    position: '50% 45%',
    tone: 'ink' as const,
    brief: 'The cadets, formed up — uniform, ranks and the school behind them',
    caption: 'The NCC cadets on Republic Day',
  },
  {
    src: bannerAssembly,
    /* ⚠ THE SCHOOL'S OWN NAME BOARD IS IN THIS FRAME and the crop must not lose
       it — it is the reason this photograph is here rather than a tidier one.
       42% holds the board and the standing rows together; 50% pushes the board
       out of the top. */
    position: '50% 42%',
    alt: 'Students and staff of Sunbeam School Ballia gathered in rows outside the school entrance, beneath the school’s name board.',
    tone: 'charcoal' as const,
    brief: 'The school together, at its own front door',
    caption: 'The senior school at the Agarsanda entrance',
  },
  {
    src: bannerInvestiture,
    alt: 'Student office-bearers of Sunbeam School Ballia on the hall steps with the house flags at the investiture ceremony.',
    position: '50% 48%',
    tone: 'ink' as const,
    brief: 'The investiture — student office-bearers take up their posts',
    caption: 'Investiture, and the house flags',
  },
  {
    src: community,
    alt: 'Senior students and staff of Sunbeam School Ballia gathered together in the school hall.',
    position: '50% 40%',
    tone: 'maroon' as const,
    brief: 'A co-curricular moment — sport, performance or competition',
    caption: 'The senior school, together',
  },
];
---

<section class="hero" data-hero aria-label="Welcome to Sunbeam School Ballia">
  <div class="hero__stage">
    {slides.map((slide, i) => (
      <div
        class="hero__slide"
        data-slide
        data-caption={slide.caption ?? slide.brief}
        data-caption-tag={slide.caption ? '' : 'Needed · A2'}
        data-active={i === 0 ? '' : undefined}
        aria-hidden={i !== 0}
      >
        <Photo
          src={slide.src}
          alt={slide.alt}
          brief={slide.brief}
          tone={slide.tone}
          position={slide.position}
          ref="A2"
          quiet
          eager={i === 0}
          sizes="100vw"
        />
        {'video' in slide && slide.video && (
          /* ⚠ NO src HERE, AND TWO CANDIDATES INSTEAD. The script picks between
             them and attaches one only once it has decided the film may run at
             all, so a phone that opts out fetches nothing. A plain src attribute
             would have every device downloading before any of that is checked.

             ⚠ data-src-sm IS THE 960-WIDE ENCODE AND PHONES GET IT. Handing a
             phone the 1600 file would be three megabytes to fill a screen that
             cannot show the detail. */
          <video
            class="hero__video"
            data-video
            data-src={slide.video}
            data-src-sm={slide.video.replace('.mp4', '-sm.mp4')}
            muted
            playsinline
            preload="none"
            tabindex="-1"
            aria-hidden="true"
          ></video>
        )}
      </div>
    ))}
    <div class="hero__tint" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="hero__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  </div>

  <div class="hero__content u-container u-on-dark">
    <div class="hero__copy">
      {/* ⚠ NO ADMISSIONS BADGE ABOVE THE HEADLINE ANY MORE. It stacked a third
          line of small type over the <h1> — badge, eyebrow, then the headline —
          and pushed the one sentence this page exists to say further down the
          fold on every phone. The admissions route is not lost: it is the
          pulsing pill in the masthead, both forms in the utility bar, and the
          whole Admissions band further down this page. */}
      {/* ⚠ THE MOTTO MOVED OUT FROM ABOVE THE HEADLINE TO BELOW THE DECK, at the
          client's request ("Duty Devotion Discipline .... I think you should add
          your tagline here", pointing under the supporting sentence). Nothing
          now stands between the top of the copy block and the <h1>, which is
          also what the note about the deleted admissions badge wanted. It is
          still the school's own motto off the crest ring — see the file header.
          There is no eyebrow in its place: the only candidates were lines this
          project would have had to invent. */}
      <p class="hero__eyebrow t-label">
        Welcome to Sunbeam School Ballia
        <span class="hero__eyebrow-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </p>
      {/* ⚠ THREE LINES, THREE TREATMENTS, AND ALL THREE ARE THE CLIENT'S DESIGN.
          "Where Ballia's Future" white at full size, "Leaders" ORANGE at full
          size, "Take Shape" white and noticeably smaller. An earlier pass had
          the orange on "Take Shape" instead; the reference the client then sent
          puts it on "Leaders" and leaves "Take Shape" white, so that is what
          this is. The first two lines carry the weight, which is still the
          hierarchy asked for in the round before.

          ⚠⚠ THREE BLOCK SPANS, NOT TWO <br>s, AND THE SPACING IS WHY. As inline
          content the small third line still sat on the FULL-SIZE line's strut —
          80.9px of leading for a 43px word — so the white band under "Leaders"
          came out roughly double the one under line 1 and the client's note was
          exactly that. A negative margin only papered over it, because the strut
          scales with the headline and the correction did not. As blocks each
          line owns its own leading and the rhythm holds at every width.

          ⚠ NO data-split HERE, DELIBERATELY. splitWords (motion-gsap.ts) rebuilds
          a heading out of inline-block masks, which would pull these three
          blocks onto one line. It never actually ran on this heading — the hero
          is above the fold and `belowFold` skips it — so the attribute was doing
          nothing but holding a loaded gun at the layout. Every other heading on
          the site still animates; this one is a fixed three-line composition. */}
      <h1 class="hero__title t-display">
        <span class="hero__line">Where Ballia's Future</span>
        <span class="hero__line hero__accent">Leaders</span>
        <span class="hero__take">Take Shape</span>
      </h1>
      {/* ⚠ VERBATIM CLIENT COPY. This sentence was supplied word for word and
          must not be re-cut. It replaced a line that interpolated
          {school.currentStrength}; the roll is not in the client's wording, so
          the interpolation is gone with it. */}
      <p class="hero__deck t-body-lg">
        A Sunbeam institution since 2013 — a CBSE school from Nursery to
        Class&nbsp;XII, where children learn, grow and prepare for the future.
      </p>
      <p class="hero__motto t-label">{school.motto}</p>
      {/* ⚠ THE HERO NO LONGER ASKS ANYONE TO APPLY, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
          It carried the two application forms — the same two that are in the
          utility bar at the top of every page, in the masthead pill, and in the
          Admissions band below. Four copies of one action on one screen is not
          four chances to convert; it is a page that will not let a reader look
          around before deciding. First contact gets the two things a parent
          actually wants first: what is taught, and where.
          ⚠ INTERNAL, SAME TAB, TRAILING SLASH. Both are pages on this site, so
          neither needs target="_blank" — and the slash matters, because
          trailingSlash: 'always' in astro.config.mjs makes the bare form a
          redirect hop. */}
      {/* ⚠ THE LABELS ARE THE REFERENCE'S; THE DESTINATIONS ARE THIS SITE'S.
          "Discover Our Campus" goes to /campus/. The play glyph beside it is the
          reference's and is decorative: it opens no lightbox, and the campus
          page is where the films live.

          ⚠ "Explore Sunbeam" GOES TO /about/history-legacy/ — "Our Journey" — AT
          THE CLIENT'S INSTRUCTION. It briefly pointed at /about/, the section
          landing page, which does build but is not where they want a reader
          sent. Note the label/route mismatch is only apparent: /about/history-
          legacy/ is titled "Our Journey" everywhere a visitor sees it (its own
          <h1>, the navbar, the footer) and the slug is kept for the redirects in
          astro.config.mjs — see the note in data/navigation.ts. */}
      <div class="hero__actions">
        <a class="btn btn--on-image-primary hero__cta" href="/about/history-legacy/">
          Explore Sunbeam
          <svg class="hero__cta-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M4 12h15M13 6l6 6-6 6" />
          </svg>
        </a>
        <a class="btn btn--on-image-secondary hero__cta" href="/campus/">
          <svg class="hero__cta-play" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="1.6" aria-hidden="true">
            <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9.2" /><path d="M10 8.6 16 12l-6 3.4V8.6Z" fill="currentColor" stroke="none" />
          </svg>
          Discover Our Campus
        </a>
      </div>

      {/* ⚠ THE CARD IS LIGHT NOW, NOT DARK GLASS, and the icons show at every
          width. It used to be a white-at-10% panel with white figures, readable
          only because the scrim behind it was heavy; the reference card is a
          near-white plate with ink figures and orange icon discs, which is what
          lets the scrim stay light. Icons are decorative, so aria-hidden — the
          figure and its label already say everything.
          ⚠⚠ THE FIGURES ARE THE SITE'S, NOT THE REFERENCE MOCK-UP'S. A design
          comp that circulated alongside this one carried "50+ Years of Legacy"
          and "100+ Dedicated Faculty". This school opened in 2013 and publishes
          neither number, so neither is here. What is here is 13+ years, the
          published roll, and the Cfore #1 ranking — all three already stand
          elsewhere on this site. Do not swap in a rounder number. */}
      <div class="hero__stats">
        <div class="hero__stat">
          <span class="hero__stat-icon" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7"
              stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <path d="M12 4 2 9l10 5 10-5-10-5Z" /><path d="M6 11.5V16c0 1.7 2.7 3 6 3s6-1.3 6-3v-4.5" />
            </svg>
          </span>
          <span class="hero__stat-body">
            <span class="hero__stat-value">13+</span>
            <span class="hero__stat-label">Years in Ballia</span>
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="hero__stat">
          <span class="hero__stat-icon" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7"
              stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <circle cx="9" cy="8" r="3.2" /><path d="M2.5 19c0-3 2.9-4.8 6.5-4.8s6.5 1.8 6.5 4.8" />
              <path d="M16.5 5.6a3.2 3.2 0 0 1 0 5.4M18 14.6c2 .7 3.5 2.2 3.5 4.4" />
            </svg>
          </span>
          <span class="hero__stat-body">
            <span class="hero__stat-value">{school.currentStrength}</span>
            <span class="hero__stat-label">Students Today</span>
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="hero__stat">
          <span class="hero__stat-icon" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7"
              stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <path d="M7 4h10v5a5 5 0 0 1-10 0V4Z" /><path d="M17 5.5h2.5a2.5 2.5 0 0 1-2.5 4M7 5.5H4.5a2.5 2.5 0 0 0 2.5 4" />
              <path d="M12 14v3M9 20h6" />
            </svg>
          </span>
          <span class="hero__stat-body">
            <span class="hero__stat-value">#1</span>
            <span class="hero__stat-label">Co-ed Day School<br />in Ballia</span>
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    {/* ⚠ THE SLIDE CAPTION IS STILL HERE, JUST NOT PAINTED. The reference has
        nothing in the bottom-right corner, so .hero__meta is display:none — but
        the markup and the script that drives it are untouched, because the
        caption is also the editorial tell for a slide still waiting on a real
        photograph ("Needed · A2"). Deleting one line of CSS brings it back. */}
    <div class="hero__meta">
      <p class="hero__caption" data-hero-caption aria-live="off">
        <span class="hero__caption-tag t-label">{slides[0].caption ? '' : 'Needed · A2'}</span>
        <span class="hero__caption-text">{slides[0].caption ?? slides[0].brief}</span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .hero {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    min-height: clamp(600px, calc(100svh - 140px), 820px);
    background: var(--sb-ink);
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  /* --- Image stage ------------------------------------------------------- */

  .hero__stage {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    z-index: 0;
  }

  .hero__slide {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    opacity: 0;
    /* Audit 1.3 — opacity only. Nothing translates, nothing scales. */
    transition: opacity var(--sb-dur-fade) ease-in-out;
  }

  .hero__slide[data-active] { opacity: 1; }

  /* Sits exactly over its own poster <Photo>, and is invisible until the script
     has it playing — so a film that never loads leaves the still in place with
     nothing to notice. */
  .hero__video {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity var(--sb-dur-fade) ease-in-out;
  }

  .hero__video[data-playing] { opacity: 1; }

  /* Square. <Photo> carries a 16px radius for the card contexts it usually sits
     in; a full-bleed banner is not one of those, and the rounded corners showed
     against the stage. */
  .hero__slide :global(.photo) { border-radius: 0; }

  /* Warm the photography toward the brand without tinting it into a filter.
     Kept light: a heavy maroon wash makes the whole frame read as sepia, and
     this school needs to look current, not archival. */
  /* ⚠ HALVED, 0.12 → 0.06. A maroon wash across the whole frame is orange over
     every pixel of the photograph, which is the other half of "the orange
     colour is becoming dominant". At 0.06 it still takes the blue edge off a
     hard midday sky without reading as a filter. */
  .hero__tint {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: rgba(61, 0, 0, 0.06);
  }

  /* Two-axis scrim.
     The stock bottom-weighted scrim assumes a photograph that darkens toward
     the base. The real campus shot is bright at BOTH ends — sky above, sunlit
     grass below — and it left the gold eyebrow effectively invisible and the
     headline marginal.
     So the vertical scrim is joined by a left-weighted one, which lands exactly
     where the copy sits (columns 1–7) and clears by the two-thirds mark, so the
     buildings on the right stay fully legible. */
  /* ⚠ LIGHTENED, AND THE WEIGHT MOVED OFF THE MIDDLE OF THE FRAME. The client's
     note was "the dark overlay is hiding the building at the back". The two
     ramps multiply, so the old pair compounded to ~0.66 at the centre of the
     image and ~0.30 over the buildings on the right — the campus was being
     dimmed everywhere, not just under the words.
     Now the horizontal ramp keeps its bite only where the copy actually is and
     clears by the 72% mark, and the vertical one is roughly halved. Measured on
     the composite: ~0.93 bottom-left (where the headline sits), ~0.42 at centre,
     ~0.18 over the right-hand buildings. The copy keeps its ground; the campus
     comes back.
     ⚠ READABILITY IS PAID FOR IN TYPE, NOT IN MORE BLACK — the headline, deck
     and motto carry their own text-shadow below. If a future photograph needs
     more contrast, reach for that first. */
  .hero__scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background:
      linear-gradient(
        to right,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.86) 0%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.74) 26%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.4) 46%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.12) 62%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0) 72%
      ),
      linear-gradient(
        to top,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.52) 0%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.24) 32%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.06) 62%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.12) 100%
      );
  }

  /* On narrow screens the copy spans the full width, so the horizontal ramp
     would leave the right-hand words sitting on bare photograph. */
  /* From tablet down the copy spans most of the width, so the horizontal ramp
     no longer covers it and the vertical one has to do all the work. */
  @media (max-width: 1023px) {
    /* The copy block reaches roughly 70% up the frame here, so the ramp has to
       hold under it — but the old curve sat at 0.8 all the way to the 78% mark
       and blacked the tablet hero out almost completely, which is the same
       complaint the desktop scrim just answered. It now releases earlier and
       ends much lighter, so the top third of the photograph reads; the type
       below carries its own shadow for the rest. */
    .hero__scrim {
      background:
        linear-gradient(
          to top,
          rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.86) 0%,
          rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.78) 50%,
          rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.54) 72%,
          rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.2) 88%,
          rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.1) 100%
        );
    }
  }

  /* --- Content ----------------------------------------------------------- */

  .hero__content {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--sb-cols), minmax(0, 1fr));
    column-gap: var(--sb-gutter);
    align-items: end;
    padding-block: var(--sb-11);
  }

  /* Asymmetric: columns 1–7 of 12. Never centred. */
  .hero__copy {
    /* ⚠ TEN COLUMNS, NOT EIGHT. .hero__meta used to hold 9–12 and is now
       display:none, so the copy is free to take the room — and it needs it: the
       headline runs to three lines and the stat card is a three-up row beside
       an icon disc each. Columns 11–12 stay clear so the card never reaches the
       right margin, which is where the reference has it end. */
    grid-column: 1 / span 10;
  }

  /* .hero__badge and .hero__badge-dot were deleted with the markup they styled
     — see the note beside the eyebrow. Dead rules for an element no page emits
     are how a stylesheet stops being readable. */

  /* Floating stat card — a near-white plate, icon disc beside each figure.
     ⚠ THE BLUR IS LOAD-BEARING. The plate is 88% white, not solid, so the
     photograph still moves underneath it; without backdrop-filter that reads as
     a flat grey rectangle dropped on the picture. Where the filter is
     unsupported the fallback is simply a slightly more opaque plate, which is
     why the alpha is 0.88 and not 0.6. */
  .hero__stats {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--sb-6);
    margin-top: var(--sb-8);
    padding: var(--sb-5) var(--sb-7);
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.88);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.55);
    border-radius: var(--sb-r-2);
    box-shadow: 0 18px 50px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.28);
    backdrop-filter: blur(14px);
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(14px);
  }

  .hero__stat {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--sb-4);
  }

  .hero__stat + .hero__stat {
    padding-left: var(--sb-6);
    border-left: 1px solid rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.12);
  }

  .hero__stat-body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1px;
  }

  /* The orange disc. This is where the reference spends its orange inside the
     card — the figures themselves stay ink, which is what keeps three big
     numerals from turning the plate into another orange block. */
  .hero__stat-icon {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 44px;
    height: 44px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--sb-lime-wash);
    color: var(--sb-violet);
  }

  .hero__stat-icon svg { width: 22px; height: 22px; display: block; }

  .hero__stat-value {
    font-family: var(--sb-font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 30px;
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    color: var(--sb-ink);
  }

  .hero__stat-label {
    font-size: var(--sb-caption);
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--sb-text-muted);
  }

  /* The motto is set in white, not gold.
     Measured over the real campus photograph, gold ran at 2.89:1 average and
     1.31:1 at worst — and the fix is not a heavier scrim: gold (#C9A227, L=0.38)
     needs a ground darker than L=0.046 to clear 4.5:1, which would mean blacking
     out the photograph entirely. Gold stays as the rule above, where it is
     decoration rather than text. */
  /* The motto, now UNDER the deck rather than over the headline. Understated on
     purpose — it is a tagline, not a second heading, so it stays at label size
     and at 78% white rather than competing with the <h1> above it. */
  .hero__motto {
    color: var(--sb-on-dark-78);
    margin: 0 0 var(--sb-8);
    text-shadow: 0 1px 8px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.55);
  }

  /* The eyebrow returns, with the reference's short orange rule after it. The
     rule is a flex child rather than a ::after so it takes the leftover space
     up to a cap — a fixed-width pseudo-element sat at a different distance from
     the text at every viewport. */
  .hero__eyebrow {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--sb-4);
    color: #fff;
    margin: 0 0 var(--sb-5);
    text-shadow: 0 1px 8px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.5);
  }

  .hero__eyebrow-rule {
    flex: 0 1 56px;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--sb-orange-tint);
  }

  .hero__title {
    color: #fff;
    /* Buys back the contrast the lightened scrim gives up, without darkening a
       single pixel of the photograph. Two stops: a tight one for edge
       definition against a bright wall, a wide soft one for the general ground. */
    text-shadow:
      0 1px 2px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.45),
      0 4px 22px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.5);
    /* FLUID, so the chosen break holds at every width rather than only at the
       two where it happened to fit.
       Line 1 is a constant 10.26em ("Where Ballia's children" at this tracking),
       and the eight-column measure is about 0.58vw. 5.2vw keeps 10.26em inside
       that at every size in the range — a fixed 76px did not: it needed 780px
       against 762px at 1240, and broke into three lines there while looking
       correct at 1200.
       No max-width: a measure's only job here would be to stop line 1 fitting,
       and `text-wrap: balance` is gone because balancing fights a break we
       have already chosen. */
    font-size: clamp(40px, 5.2vw, 76px);
    max-width: none;
    margin-bottom: var(--sb-6);
  }

  /* ⚠ --sb-orange-tint, NOT --sb-violet. The primary orange (#c93c0a) is a
     light-ground colour; over a photograph it goes muddy and loses the contrast
     that makes this line the accent it is meant to be. */
  .hero__accent {
    color: var(--sb-orange-tint);
  }

  /* ⚠ 0.58em — THE NUMBER THAT CARRIES THE HIERARCHY. "Take Shape" reads as a
     smaller third line rather than a continuation of the headline, which was
     the client's note two rounds ago and is still true of the reference. It is
     white here, because the reference spends the headline's orange on
     "Leaders"; it also drops to weight 600, so it settles rather than shouts. */
  /* Each line is its own block box, so each carries its own leading — see the
     note beside the markup. */
  .hero__line { display: block; }

  .hero__take {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.58em;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    /* 1.1 of 0.58em, not 1.08 of 1em: the leading that lands this line the same
       optical distance under "Leaders" as "Leaders" sits under line 1. */
    line-height: 1.1;
    margin-top: 0.04em;
  }

  .hero__deck {
    color: var(--sb-on-dark-90);
    max-width: 46ch;
    margin-bottom: var(--sb-5);
    text-shadow: 0 1px 10px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.5);
  }

  .hero__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--sb-4);
  }

  .hero__cta {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--sb-3);
  }

  .hero__cta-icon { width: 18px; height: 18px; flex: 0 0 auto; }
  .hero__cta-play { width: 22px; height: 22px; flex: 0 0 auto; }

  /* --- Meta: editorial caption + controls, bottom right ------------------ */

  /* See the note beside the markup: hidden, not deleted. */
  .hero__meta { display: none; }

  .hero__caption {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-end;
    gap: var(--sb-2);
    padding-right: var(--sb-5);
    border-right: 2px solid var(--sb-gold);
    transition: opacity var(--sb-dur-fade) ease-in-out;
  }

  .hero__caption.is-fading { opacity: 0; }

  .hero__caption-tag { color: var(--sb-gold); }

  .hero__caption-text {
    font-size: var(--sb-caption);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--sb-on-dark-78);
    max-width: 34ch;
  }


  /* --- Tablet ------------------------------------------------------------- */

  @media (max-width: 1023px) {
    .hero__copy { grid-column: 1 / span 10; }
    .hero__title { max-width: none; }
  }

  /* Below ~900 the caption competes with the deck for the same optical line. */
  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    .hero__caption-text { display: none; }
    .hero__caption { border-right: 0; padding-right: 0; }
    .hero__caption-tag { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5); }
  }

  /* --- Mobile: 4:5 crop, stacked actions, controls below copy ------------- */

  /* ⚠ THE BREAK IS NO LONGER DROPPED ON NARROW SCREENS, and that rule is gone
     rather than tightened. It existed because "Take Shape" used to be a plate
     set at the full headline size: below ~480 the first phrase could not fit on
     one line anyway, so the manual break was dropped and the whole thing wrapped
     as one heading. "Take Shape" is now a smaller second line, so the break IS
     the hierarchy — without it the phrase ran inline after "Leaders" at 390,
     which is exactly what the client asked not to happen. The first phrase wraps
     naturally over two lines at that width; it no longer needs the break. */

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    /* ⚠⚠ ON MOBILE THE FILM IS A BLOCK AT THE TOP, NOT A BACKGROUND.
       This is the difference between this block and the desktop layout above,
       and it is the whole point of it.

       DESKTOP: .hero__stage is position:absolute inset:0, so the film sits
       BEHIND everything and the copy is laid over it through a scrim.

       MOBILE: the stage is taken out of that and put back into normal flow as a
       fixed-height band, and the copy sits BELOW it on the hero's own solid
       --sb-ink. Nothing is layered over the film and nothing of the film runs
       behind the headline.

       An earlier pass did this the other way — full-bleed film with a gradient
       over the lower two-thirds. It technically showed the campus, but the
       building stayed visible behind the headline and the client's note was
       exactly that: the video should be at the top with the content below it,
       not playing underneath the text.

       ⚠ --hero-band IS THE ONLY DIMENSION TO TOUCH. The stage height, the fade
       into the solid ground and the play button all derive from it. */
    .hero {
      --hero-band: clamp(190px, 30svh, 250px);
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: stretch;
      /* Height comes from band + copy now, not from a viewport clamp — a fixed
         min-height would leave dead ground under the stat card on tall phones. */
      min-height: 0;
      background: var(--sb-ink);
    }

    .hero__stage {
      position: relative;
      inset: auto;
      flex: 0 0 auto;
      width: 100%;
      height: var(--hero-band);
    }

    /* Only a soft landing at the bottom edge of the film, so it meets the solid
       ground without a hard seam. The film itself is left completely unwashed —
       covering it is what the previous version got wrong. */
    .hero__scrim {
      background: linear-gradient(
        to bottom,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0) 0,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0) 58%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.5) 86%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 1) 100%
      );
    }

    /* The maroon wash exists to sit a scrim on a photograph. There is no scrim
       over the film here, so it would just dirty it. */
    .hero__tint { background: none; }

    .hero__content {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: stretch;
      padding-block: var(--sb-7) var(--sb-8);
      gap: var(--sb-6);
    }


    /* ⚠ SAME HIERARCHY ON A PHONE, which is the client's point 9. The headline
       caps at 46px here, so "Take Shape" holding 0.42em of that would fall to
       19px and read as body copy — it takes a floor of its own instead, still
       comfortably under the line above it. */
    .hero__take {
      font-size: clamp(19px, 5.2vw, 25px);
      /* Measured, not guessed: at 0.28em the band under "Leaders" ran 18px
         against 12px under line 1 — the same unevenness the desktop rule just
         fixed, reintroduced by this override. */
      margin-top: 0.1em;
    }

    .hero__motto {
      margin-bottom: var(--sb-6);
    }

    /* ⚠ THE TITLE IS DELIBERATELY SMALLER THAN THE FLUID DESKTOP RULE. At 5.2vw
       the headline ran to three very deep lines on a phone and pushed the stat
       card off the fold entirely — the reference has the figures visible. This
       caps it without touching the desktop curve. */
    .hero__title {
      max-width: none;
      margin-bottom: var(--sb-4);
      font-size: clamp(34px, 9.4vw, 46px);
      line-height: 1.04;
    }

    .hero__deck {
      max-width: none;
      /* The motto now sits between the deck and the buttons and carries the
         gap; the deck only needs to clear it. */
      margin-bottom: var(--sb-4);
      font-size: clamp(15px, 4.2vw, 17px);
    }

    /* The card keeps the light plate it has on desktop, but stacks the icon
       above the figure again: three icon-beside-figure rows will not fit across
       a 320px phone without the labels crushing. */
    .hero__stats {
      display: flex;
      width: 100%;
      gap: 0;
      padding: var(--sb-5) var(--sb-3);
      margin-top: var(--sb-6);
      background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.94);
      border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6);
      border-radius: var(--sb-r-2);
      backdrop-filter: blur(10px);
      -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(10px);
    }

    /* Equal thirds, so the three-word label under #1 cannot widen its column
       and squeeze the other two. */
    .hero__stat {
      flex: 1 1 0;
      min-width: 0;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 4px;
      text-align: center;
      padding-inline: 6px;
    }

    .hero__stat + .hero__stat {
      padding-left: 6px;
      border-left: 1px solid rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.12);
    }

    .hero__stat-body {
      align-items: center;
      text-align: center;
    }

    .hero__stat-icon {
      width: 34px;
      height: 34px;
      margin-bottom: 2px;
    }

    .hero__stat-icon svg { width: 18px; height: 18px; }
    .hero__stat-value { font-size: clamp(19px, 5.4vw, 24px); }
    .hero__stat-label {
      font-size: clamp(9.5px, 2.7vw, 11px);
      line-height: 1.25;
      text-wrap: balance;
    }


    .hero__actions { flex-direction: column; }
    .hero__actions .btn { width: 100%; }

    .hero__eyebrow { margin-bottom: var(--sb-4); }
  }

  /* Below ~400 the eyebrow wraps to two lines and the rule ends up alone on the
     second, reading as a stray dash rather than as part of the line. */
  @media (max-width: 400px) {
    .hero__eyebrow-rule { display: none; }

  }
</style>

<script>
  const hero = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-hero]');

  if (hero) {
    const slides = Array.from(hero.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-slide]'));
    const caption = hero.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-hero-caption]');
    const captionText = caption?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.hero__caption-text');
    const captionTag = caption?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.hero__caption-tag');

    // A real photograph gets an editorial caption; a slot still awaiting one
    // keeps showing its shot brief under a "Needed" tag.
    const captions = slides.map((slide) => ({
      text: slide.dataset.caption ?? '',
      tag: slide.dataset.captionTag ?? '',
    }));

    const INTERVAL = 4500;
    let index = 0;
    let timer: number | undefined;

    const reduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');
    // Auto-advance is desktop-only and motion-respecting (docs/06 §03 Mobile).
    const mayAutoplay = () =>
      !reduced.matches && window.matchMedia('(min-width: 768px)').matches;

    /* ⚠ THE FILM SLIDES RUN ON THEIR OWN CLOCK, NOT THE 4.5s INTERVAL. A drone
       clip is twenty seconds; cutting it off a fifth of the way through is worse
       than not having it. So while a video slide is playing the interval is
       stopped, and `ended` is what advances the rotation.

       ⚠ AND EVERY PATH OUT OF HERE MUST STILL ADVANCE. If the film cannot play —
       narrow viewport, reduced motion, a browser refusing muted autoplay, a 404
       on the file — the interval has to take over or the hero stops dead on
       slide one with a still. That is why the failure branch calls start(). */
    const filmOf = (i: number) => slides[i].querySelector<HTMLVideoElement>('[data-video]');

    /* ⚠ THE FILM GATE IS NOT mayAutoplay(). They were the same test once and
       that is why the films never ran on a phone: mayAutoplay() is the rule for
       the PHOTO INTERVAL, and that rule is deliberately desktop-only
       (docs/06 §03 Mobile). The client wants the drone footage on mobile too, so
       the film has its own gate — and the photo interval keeps its old
       behaviour, untouched, on both.
       ⚠ IT IS NOT "PLAY EVERYWHERE" EITHER. Data Saver and a 2G-class
       connection opt out, because a parent on a metered handset should not
       spend a megabyte on an establishing shot they did not ask for. Reduced
       motion opts out as before. In every one of those cases the poster still
       carries the slide. */
    const link = (navigator as any).connection;
    const thrifty = () =>
      link?.saveData === true || /(^|-)2g$/.test(link?.effectiveType ?? '');
    const mayPlayFilm = () => !reduced.matches && !thrifty();
    const narrow = () => !window.matchMedia('(min-width: 768px)').matches;

    const attach = (video: HTMLVideoElement) => {
      if (video.src) return;
      const src = narrow() ? video.dataset.srcSm ?? video.dataset.src : video.dataset.src;
      if (src) video.src = src;
    };

    const rest = (i: number) => {
      const film = filmOf(i);
      if (!film) return;
      film.removeAttribute('data-playing');
      film.pause();
      /* ⚠ NO currentTime = 0 HERE. Seeking a finished clip back to the start
         makes the browser re-request the file — a second download of the whole
         video, which showed up as hero-1-sm.mp4 being fetched twice in one
         pass. roll() already rewinds immediately before it plays, which is the
         only moment the position actually matters. */
    };

    const roll = (i: number) => {
      const film = filmOf(i);
      if (!film || !mayPlayFilm()) return false;
      attach(film);
      stop();
      film.currentTime = 0;
      film
        .play()
        .then(() => film.setAttribute('data-playing', ''))
        .catch(() => {
          /* Autoplay refused. Leave the poster showing and hand the slide back
             to the timer rather than stranding the rotation here. */
          film.removeAttribute('data-playing');
          start();
        });
      return true;
    };

    const show = (next: number) => {
      if (next === index) return;
      slides[index].removeAttribute('data-active');
      slides[index].setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      rest(index);

      index = (next + slides.length) % slides.length;

      slides[index].setAttribute('data-active', '');
      slides[index].setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'false');
      if (!roll(index)) start();

      // The caption belongs to the image, so it cross-fades with it.
      if (caption && captionText && captionTag) {
        caption.classList.add('is-fading');
        window.setTimeout(() => {
          captionText.textContent = captions[index].text;
          captionTag.textContent = captions[index].tag;
          caption.classList.remove('is-fading');
        }, 450);
      }
    };

    const stop = () => {
      if (timer) window.clearInterval(timer);
      timer = undefined;
    };

    const start = () => {
      stop();
      if (!mayAutoplay()) return;
      /* ⚠ A PLAYING FILM OWNS THE CLOCK — DO NOT ARM THE INTERVAL OVER IT. Every
         hover out of the hero calls start(), so without this guard leaving the
         mouse would set a 4.5s timer running underneath a twenty-second clip and
         chop it off four seconds in. `ended` is what advances a film slide. */
      if (filmOf(index)?.hasAttribute('data-playing')) return;
      timer = window.setInterval(() => show(index + 1), INTERVAL);
    };

    /* ⚠ THIS IS NOW THE ONLY WAY TO STOP THE ROTATION. The pause/play button
       was removed at the client's request, so hovering the hero or tabbing into
       it is all that halts autoplay. Autoplay is already off entirely under
       prefers-reduced-motion and below 768px, which is what keeps this
       defensible — do not widen where it runs without restoring a control. */
    hero.addEventListener('mouseenter', stop);
    hero.addEventListener('mouseleave', start);
    hero.addEventListener('focusin', stop);
    hero.addEventListener('focusout', start);

    // Swipe — the only way to advance on mobile.
    let touchX = 0;
    hero.addEventListener(
      'touchstart',
      (event) => { touchX = event.changedTouches[0].clientX; },
      { passive: true }
    );
    hero.addEventListener(
      'touchend',
      (event) => {
        const delta = event.changedTouches[0].clientX - touchX;
        if (Math.abs(delta) > 48) show(index + (delta < 0 ? 1 : -1));
      },
      { passive: true }
    );

    slides.forEach((_, i) => {
      const film = filmOf(i);
      film?.addEventListener('ended', () => {
        film.removeAttribute('data-playing');
        show(index + 1);
      });
      /* Same failure mode by another route: a clip that 404s or fails to decode
         never reaches `ended`. Hand the slide back to the interval rather than
         leaving the hero parked on a still forever. */
      film?.addEventListener('error', () => {
        film.removeAttribute('data-playing');
        start();
      });
    });

    /* ⚠⚠ DO NOT PAUSE THE FILM ON HOVER. IT DEADLOCKS THE WHOLE ROTATION.
       An earlier version did exactly that, to match the timer's pause-on-hover.
       But a cursor resting anywhere over the hero — which is where it sits while
       anyone is actually looking at the page — paused the clip about a second
       in, and a paused clip never fires `ended`, and `ended` is the only thing
       that advances a film slide. The hero stopped dead on slide one and no
       photograph was ever reached. It reproduces instantly: load the page and
       leave the mouse over the banner.

       So hover governs the INTERVAL only, which is what it was written for. The
       film plays through and hands off when it ends, hovered or not. */

    reduced.addEventListener('change', start);

    /* Slide one is a film, so the opening move is to roll it; start() is the
       fallback for every case where it will not run. */
    if (!roll(index)) start();
  }
</script>
